<h1>Kochański's Workshop</h1>
<p class="lede">Adam Adamandy Kochański spent forty years on a single question: how to turn something elusive — the circumference of a circle, an oscillation, the movement of the Sun — into a number that can be repeated. The four instruments above can be operated.</p>

<p>They are not illustrations to a text. The <a href="/en/compass/">compass</a> strikes the construction step by step and gives its error at any radius — from micrometres on a clock face to hundreds of metres at the radius of the Earth. The <a href="/en/pendulum/">pendulum</a> sends a ship on Richer's voyage to Cayenne and shows how far two clocks drift from the truth over seventy-four days. The <a href="/en/cipher/">cipher</a> enciphers, deciphers and breaks text by letter frequency. The <a href="/en/gnomon/">gnomon</a> puts the Sun above the Wilanów wall and makes the shadow answer in three reckonings of the hour at once. Each of them computes for real, from scratch every time — none replays a recording.</p>

<h2>From the source, and ours</h2>
<p>A pair of markers stands beside each instrument's name. The first says that part of what you see rests on a period account. The second names what we add ourselves — simplified geometry, a modern error model, a reading of intent. The marker explains itself: point at it and it says what exactly it covers, and which register entry still has the matter open.</p>

<p>That division is the spine here, not a footnote. What we do not know is kept apart, in the <a href="/en/agenda/">register of open questions</a>; how a doubt was settled and why goes into the decision register, kept in the open from the first day. The <a href="/en/sources/">list of sources</a> links to scans wherever the licence allows it — and says so plainly where it does not.</p>

<h2>The man behind the instruments</h2>
<p>The <a href="/en/biography/">life</a> is a secondary layer here, and deliberately so: this site tells the problem Kochański worked on, not the order of his moves from city to city. The timeline gives only what is needed to place the instruments in time, with the points where the scholarship disagrees marked as such.</p>

<h2>Yours to take</h2>
<p>Each instrument works on its own and has its own address for embedding on another site. It needs nothing but a browser, stays inside a page-weight budget checked at every build, and without scripting falls back to a legible document with a text description and the formulae. The site is a demonstration version: the parts marked as ours await refinement once the source enquiry is done.</p>
